---
import ExperienceLayout from "../../layouts/ExperienceLayout.astro";
import { experiencePaths } from "../../features/experience/routes";
import NewsHeader from "../../features/experience/world/NewsHeader.astro";
import NewsFooter from "../../features/experience/world/NewsFooter.astro";
import "../../features/experience/world/news.css";

const paths = experiencePaths(import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
---

<ExperienceLayout
  title="Public does not mean permission — The Civic Margin"
  description="A sourced explainer on data availability, aggregation, and privacy protections, accompanying the fictional BROOD surveillance experience."
  world="news"
>
  <div class="sheet">
    <NewsHeader active="explainer" />
    <main id="main-content">
      <article>
        <header class="article-header">
          <p class="rubric">
            <span class="fiction-tag"
              >Sourced explainer / real-world background</span
            ><span>Data markets · privacy frameworks</span>
          </p>
          <h1>Public does not mean permission.</h1>
          <p class="deck">
            An ordinary trace can become part of an extraordinary picture. The
            important questions concern how that picture was assembled, who can
            use it, and what control remains with its subject.
          </p>
          <div class="byline">
            <p>By the Civic Margin editorial desk</p><p>
              Source notes checked <time datetime="2026-10-31"
                >31 October 2026</time
              >
            </p>
          </div>
        </header>
        <div class="article-layout">
          <div class="article-body">
            <div class="reality-note">
              <p>
                The Civic Margin and BROOD are fictional. This explainer
                discusses real sources, linked where used. The invented
                investigation is not evidence about a real surveillance company.
              </p>
            </div>
            <p>
              The BROOD story turns information into an atmosphere: an empty
              room, an attentive machine, and an institution pleased with the
              completeness of its records. The real question underneath it is
              less theatrical. What does the ability to obtain information
              entitle its holder to do?
            </p>
            <h2>A browser readout is one part of the picture.</h2>
            <p>
              The observation console reflects browser-reported settings and
              events inside its own page: local time, device characteristics,
              selected views, and intervals without interaction. These
              observations remain in the current document. It does not retrieve
              someone’s accounts or buy a commercial dossier. Its optional
              network panel reports information Cloudflare supplies about the
              current request, labels that source, and does not invent a
              location when readings are unavailable.
            </p>
            <p>
              Those design choices matter to the piece. A technical reading is
              not proof of identity, and a selected channel is not proof of a
              person’s thoughts. Treating either as a complete portrait is part
              of the fictional institution’s confidence. The artwork invites the
              visitor to question that confidence.
            </p>
            <h2>Availability has more than one meaning.</h2>
            <p>
              The FTC explains that people-search services assemble personal
              information into reports for sale. Their inputs can include public
              records and other sources. The agency also describes opt-out
              routes and their limits: removal from a service does not erase the
              underlying public record, and information may later reappear. <a
                href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-people-search-sites-sell-your-information"
                >FTC consumer guidance</a
              >
            </p>
            <p>
              That is different from saying that everybody’s data is freely
              available online. Some information is public; other information is
              bought, licensed, supplied under particular conditions, or exposed
              against expectations. Collapsing those differences into “it was
              already there” makes a convenient corporate argument. It does not
              answer the ethical questions about combining records or changing
              their purpose.
            </p>
            <blockquote class="pullquote">
              <p>
                The question is not only what can be found. It is who gets to
                turn finding it into power.
              </p><cite>Editorial interpretation</cite>
            </blockquote>
            <h2>Different frameworks, uneven reach.</h2>
            <p>
              The Congressional Research Service’s August 2025 report describes
              U.S. federal consumer privacy law as sectoral, covering areas such
              as health, finance, and children’s information, alongside broader
              state laws. That is a patchwork of protections, rather than an
              absence of privacy law. Which rules apply depends on the relevant
              law and circumstances. <a
                href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48667"
                >CRS: Preemption and Privacy Law</a
              >
            </p>
            <p>
              The EU’s GDPR uses a broader framework for covered personal-data
              processing. The European Data Protection Board identifies
              requirements including lawful and fair processing, transparency,
              defined purposes, limited collection and retention, and
              accountability. The framework does not prohibit every use of data
              or guarantee that misuse never occurs. <a
                href="https://www.edpb.europa.eu/topics/key-gdpr-concepts/basic-principles_en"
                >EDPB: Basic principles</a
              >
            </p>
            <p>
              For the fictional BROOD, the gaps and boundaries become a sales
              language: continuity, usefulness, confidence. For a reader, they
              should become questions. What was the original purpose? Who
              authorized the next use? What happens when the records are wrong?
              Who can inspect the institution that inspects everyone else?
            </p>
            <h2>Leave with a question—and a real resource.</h2>
            <p>
              Privacy should not depend entirely on each person finding every
              intermediary. Individual tools can still offer practical starting
              points. Their coverage and conditions differ, so use the official
              explanations to understand what each can change.
            </p>
            <ul class="action-links">
              <li>
                <a
                  href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-people-search-sites-sell-your-information"
                  >Understand people-search opt-outs ↗</a
                ><span
                  >The FTC describes removal options and why they may not remove
                  every copy or source.</span
                >
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://privacy.ca.gov/drop/how-drop-works/"
                  >California residents: learn about DROP ↗</a
                ><span
                  >The official platform supports deletion requests to
                  registered data brokers. Its scope has exceptions, including
                  information supplied directly to a business; it does not erase
                  the entire internet.</span
                >
              </li>
              <li>
                <a
                  href="https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/consumers/internet-telecoms/data-protection-online-privacy/"
                  >Read the EU’s explanation of data rights ↗</a
                ><span
                  >Your Europe describes rights such as access and erasure, with
                  conditions and exceptions.</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              The activism in this piece is an invitation to ask for
              explanations that can be checked. A procurement promise should
              have a retention policy behind it. A claim of public benefit
              should have a way to challenge harm. And an institution asking to
              see more should expect to be questioned in return.
            </p>
            <p>
              Read the <a href={paths.investigation}
                >fictional BROOD investigation</a
              >, then compare it with <a href={paths.confidence}
                >the company’s Public Confidence response</a
              >. The contradiction is deliberate. The real-world references are
              there to make the next conversation more specific.
            </p>
          </div>
          <aside
            class="article-aside"
            aria-label="Sources and editorial context"
          >
            <section class="aside-block">
              <h2>What this page is</h2><p>
                A sourced background explainer inside a fictional publication.
                It separates the artwork’s invented allegations from documented
                issues and provides official starting points for further
                reading.
              </p><p>
                Source dates describe the referenced material. They are not a
                claim that a later law or enforcement development has been ruled
                out.
              </p>
            </section>
            <section class="aside-block">
              <h2>Primary-source notebook</h2><ol class="source-list">
                <li>
                  <a
                    href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/what-know-about-people-search-sites-sell-your-information"
                    >FTC · People-search services</a
                  ><span class="source-date"
                    >Consumer guidance · checked 31 October 2026</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48667"
                    >CRS · Preemption and Privacy Law</a
                  ><span class="source-date"
                    >Report R48667 · 29 August 2025</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <a
                    href="https://www.edpb.europa.eu/topics/key-gdpr-concepts/basic-principles_en"
                    >EDPB · Basic principles</a
                  ><span class="source-date"
                    >Official guidance · checked 31 October 2026</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <a href="https://privacy.ca.gov/drop/how-drop-works/"
                    >California · How DROP works</a
                  ><span class="source-date"
                    >Official platform guidance · checked 31 October 2026</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li>
                  <a
                    href="https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/consumers/internet-telecoms/data-protection-online-privacy/"
                    >Your Europe · Data rights</a
                  ><span class="source-date"
                    >Official consumer guidance · checked 31 October 2026</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ol>
            </section>
            <section class="aside-block">
              <h2>In the fictional record</h2><h3 class="related-title">
                The company says the archive is a form of care.
              </h3><p>
                Our invented investigation follows the difference between
                collecting a record and being accountable for it.
              </p><a href={paths.investigation}>Read the BROOD story →</a><a
                href={paths.company}>Visit the fictional company ↗</a
              >
            </section>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>
    <NewsFooter active="explainer" />
  </div>
</ExperienceLayout>
